WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.583 --> 00:00:02.001
I don't know about you,
but I've noticed

00:00:02.085 --> 00:00:03.920
a very welcome uptick recently

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:05.463
in the number of fantasy books

00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:08.466
inspired by
West African folklore.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.052
The Afrofuturism and
African futurism movements

00:00:11.136 --> 00:00:12.762
have paved the way
for Black authors

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:15.390
across the diaspora
to build worlds

00:00:15.473 --> 00:00:18.101
and tell stories influenced
by their heritage.

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.937
Thanks to the hit success
of books like Tomi Adeyemi's

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:24.315
"Children of Blood and Bone,"
wider audiences are finally

00:00:24.399 --> 00:00:28.278
getting introduced to the orisha
and spirits of Yoruba lore.

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:31.281
But who are these deities,
and where do they come from?

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:33.950
[upbeat music]

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:37.954
In Adeyemi's novel,
the main character is part

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:40.957
of the Iku clan that
worships the deity Oya

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.710
and traditionally has
powers over life and death.

00:00:43.793 --> 00:00:46.921
Oya is a real figure
from Yoruba mythology,

00:00:47.005 --> 00:00:49.632
a collection of stories
from the widespread tribe

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:51.718
that speaks the Yoruba language.

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:55.096
Oya is one of many
orisha, or deities,

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:58.099
who can move between the
human and divine realms.

00:00:58.183 --> 00:00:59.934
With control over the wind,

00:01:00.018 --> 00:01:02.228
a reputation as
a fierce warrior,

00:01:02.312 --> 00:01:04.439
and a responsibility
to watch over

00:01:04.522 --> 00:01:06.566
the threshold of life and death,

00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:09.486
Oya is one of the more
formidable Yoruba deities,

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:12.655
but she is by no means
the most powerful.

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:14.657
According to Yoruba mythology,

00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:17.243
the universe and all
of the forces therein

00:01:17.327 --> 00:01:19.704
were created by
a single supreme being,

00:01:19.788 --> 00:01:22.290
most often known as Olofin.

00:01:22.373 --> 00:01:25.335
Also known as
Olorun or Olodamare,

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:28.421
this genderless creator
god is all-knowing,

00:01:28.505 --> 00:01:30.632
all-powerful and eternal.

00:01:30.715 --> 00:01:32.884
It might be tempting
to compare Olofin

00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:36.387
to the Abrahamic God, but
they are a unique being

00:01:36.471 --> 00:01:38.431
and no other deity is like them.

00:01:38.515 --> 00:01:41.601
In the beginning of time,
Olofin crafted the universe

00:01:41.684 --> 00:01:43.603
and invented day and night.

00:01:43.686 --> 00:01:45.647
They gave divine
energy called ase

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.607
to all the objects and beings

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:51.361
and breathed life, or emi,
into every living thing.

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:54.906
They did not, however, want
to manage every living thing,

00:01:54.989 --> 00:01:58.284
so Olofin created the orisha
to act as intermediaries

00:01:58.368 --> 00:02:01.162
between Orun, the
spiritual heavenly realm,

00:02:01.246 --> 00:02:04.916
and Aye, the world of tangible,
visible things.

00:02:04.999 --> 00:02:08.336
There are hundreds of
orisha in Yoruba folklore,

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:10.755
though it may be impossible
to give an exact count

00:02:10.839 --> 00:02:13.716
because their characteristics
are malleable.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.929
A single orisha's name,
gender, and parentage can vary

00:02:18.012 --> 00:02:20.431
depending on when and
where you hear the story,

00:02:20.515 --> 00:02:23.685
though their core traits
remain largely unchanged.

00:02:23.768 --> 00:02:27.188
Some of the orisha, like
Obatala and Orunmila,

00:02:27.272 --> 00:02:29.482
are primordial figures
who were involved

00:02:29.566 --> 00:02:31.109
in the creation of the world.

00:02:31.192 --> 00:02:35.196
Others like Oduduwa or
Shango, were mortal ancestors

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.366
whose deeds led them to be
deified into the religion.

00:02:38.449 --> 00:02:40.785
And there were many
orisha who personified

00:02:40.869 --> 00:02:43.788
aspects of nature like
the spirits who embody

00:02:43.872 --> 00:02:46.499
the numerous rivers
and lakes in the world.

00:02:46.583 --> 00:02:49.836
Before the world was created,
Olofin and the orisha

00:02:49.919 --> 00:02:51.963
lived in the sky around
a young baobab tree.

00:02:52.046 --> 00:02:55.091
Below them stretched
a vast ocean

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:57.176
ruled by the orisha Olokun.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:00.179
Most of the orisha were
content to explore the sky

00:03:00.263 --> 00:03:03.641
and enjoy a life of luxury
around their baobab tree,

00:03:03.725 --> 00:03:04.976
but Obatala wanted more.

00:03:05.059 --> 00:03:08.605
He looked at the vast
ocean below and thought,

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:10.857
"Hmm, I could make
something with that."

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.775
So he asked Olofin
if he could build

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:15.236
a resting place
for living things.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.655
Olofin gave him
permission to make land,

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:20.575
but left it to Obatala
to figure out how.

00:03:20.658 --> 00:03:22.118
Obatala went to Orunmila,

00:03:22.201 --> 00:03:24.454
the orisha of
prophecy, for guidance.

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:27.749
Orunmila, whose power
of divination made him

00:03:27.832 --> 00:03:31.044
the sole witness to Olofin's
creation of the universe,

00:03:31.127 --> 00:03:34.339
told Obatala to fashion
a long gold chain,

00:03:34.422 --> 00:03:37.383
fill a snail shell with
sand, and gather a chicken

00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:39.052
and a date palm nut.

00:03:39.135 --> 00:03:42.180
Obatala traveled the sky,
asking the other orisha

00:03:42.263 --> 00:03:45.141
for their spare gold, which
he forged into a chain

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:47.602
to carry him to Olofin's ocean.

00:03:47.685 --> 00:03:50.104
When Obatala climbed to
the end of the chain,

00:03:50.188 --> 00:03:52.982
he found that he still
had some distance to go.

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:56.819
From his position in the
sky, Orunmila told Obatala

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:59.322
to pour sand from his
shell onto the ocean

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:01.741
and immediately
drop the chicken.

00:04:01.824 --> 00:04:05.119
When it landed, the chicken
began to kick the sand around

00:04:05.203 --> 00:04:08.706
until it formed a huge land
mass with hills and valleys.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:11.209
Obatala jumped
down from his chain

00:04:11.292 --> 00:04:12.961
and planted his date palm nut,

00:04:13.044 --> 00:04:15.797
which immediately
sprouted into a full tree.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.133
That spot became Ife,
the legendary cradle

00:04:19.217 --> 00:04:21.761
of Yoruba civilization
that archeologists believe

00:04:21.844 --> 00:04:25.098
was an urban center by 800 C.E.

00:04:25.181 --> 00:04:27.934
Obatala made some wine
from the fruit of his tree,

00:04:28.017 --> 00:04:31.187
and after partaking in
perhaps too many libations,

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.856
began to mold
people out of clay.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.609
Many of the figurines
were imperfect,

00:04:36.693 --> 00:04:39.195
with missing limbs
or unintended bends,

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:41.906
but Olofin breathed
emi into them anyway.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:44.659
When Obatala saw his
creations come to life,

00:04:44.742 --> 00:04:48.413
he vowed never to drink again
and assumed responsibility

00:04:48.496 --> 00:04:51.332
for protecting humans
with physical differences.

00:04:51.416 --> 00:04:53.001
In some stories,
Obatala got drunk

00:04:53.084 --> 00:04:56.546
before he could complete
his task of making land,

00:04:56.629 --> 00:04:59.549
and his younger brother,
the orisha Oduduwa,

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:01.175
took over for him.

00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:03.136
Regardless of who made the land,

00:05:03.219 --> 00:05:06.431
Yoruba lore tells
of Oduduwa, or Odua,

00:05:06.514 --> 00:05:08.224
the first king of Ife from whom

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:10.435
the Yoruba people are descended.

00:05:10.518 --> 00:05:13.896
Olokun was furious that her
ocean had been encroached upon

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:17.567
without her permission--not
even a heads up from Obatala--

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.319
so she sent great waves
to flood the land.

00:05:20.403 --> 00:05:23.656
Those lucky enough to reach
high hills besieged Eshu,

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.325
the messenger orisha
of roads and doors,

00:05:26.409 --> 00:05:28.202
to intervene on their behalf.

00:05:28.286 --> 00:05:30.580
Eshu told Orunmila
about the flood

00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:33.666
and he used Obatala's
golden chain to climb down

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:37.045
and cast spells until
Olokun's water retreated.

00:05:37.128 --> 00:05:39.213
Notice that the
people begged Eshu

00:05:39.297 --> 00:05:42.383
to take care of the flood,
not the supreme creator.

00:05:42.467 --> 00:05:45.428
As the messenger orisha,
Eshu stands watch

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:48.514
at the boundary between
the realms Orun and Aye,

00:05:48.598 --> 00:05:50.975
the two halves of
the Yoruba cosmos.

00:05:51.059 --> 00:05:54.645
Eshu ferries notes and
sacrifices across the threshold

00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:56.981
and looks over ritual practices.

00:05:57.065 --> 00:06:00.234
According to a Yoruba saying,
If Eshu is not acknowledged

00:06:00.318 --> 00:06:02.862
during a ritual, then
life is the bailing

00:06:02.945 --> 00:06:04.530
of waters with a sieve.

00:06:04.614 --> 00:06:07.158
In other words, if you're
not going to honor Eshu,

00:06:07.241 --> 00:06:08.451
why even bother?

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:10.995
This makes even more
sense when you consider

00:06:11.079 --> 00:06:14.082
that Eshu is the trickster
of Yoruba mythology.

00:06:14.165 --> 00:06:17.376
Ritual sacrifice and prayer
are just two of the ways

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:21.005
mortals can connect to the
orishas' wisdom and power.

00:06:21.089 --> 00:06:23.466
The other is through
divination that lets humans see

00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:26.511
the universal forces that
influence their lives.

00:06:26.594 --> 00:06:29.013
So Orunmila is the
other orisha who sits

00:06:29.097 --> 00:06:32.600
between Orun and Aye
as Olofin intended.

00:06:32.683 --> 00:06:34.977
Now, I don't know from
personal experience,

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:37.313
but any parent can tell
you that just because

00:06:37.396 --> 00:06:39.065
you give a being
life doesn't mean

00:06:39.148 --> 00:06:41.109
they'll always follow
your directions.

00:06:41.192 --> 00:06:44.612
The orisha are no different,
and in one Yoruba myth,

00:06:44.695 --> 00:06:47.698
they decided they were tired
of listening to Olofin.

00:06:47.782 --> 00:06:50.785
What could a being who lived
so far away from the world

00:06:50.868 --> 00:06:53.037
possibly know about
how to run it?

00:06:53.121 --> 00:06:54.956
They believed they
could do a better job

00:06:55.039 --> 00:06:58.501
maintaining the world without
Olofin's pesky influence.

00:06:58.584 --> 00:07:01.337
Instead of punishing the
orisha for their disrespect,

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:04.257
Olofin held the rains
back from the world.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:08.010
Rivers and lakes evaporated
away, crops withered,

00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:11.305
and animals and humans
alike died of thirst.

00:07:11.389 --> 00:07:12.807
They cried to the orisha,

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:14.851
as they had always
done when distressed,

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:17.436
"What have we done
to anger you?"

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.021
The humans never
imagined that it was

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:22.108
the distant creator god
who was causing their pain.

00:07:22.191 --> 00:07:24.861
The orisha begged for
Olofin's forgiveness,

00:07:24.944 --> 00:07:28.114
but their cries couldn't
carry far enough to be heard.

00:07:28.197 --> 00:07:31.242
Many of the orisha tried to
reach Olofin's heavenly home

00:07:31.325 --> 00:07:33.286
but they tired before
they reached it.

00:07:33.369 --> 00:07:36.038
Beautiful Oshun, the
youngest of the orisha

00:07:36.122 --> 00:07:39.959
who looks after the world's
sweet waters, wanted to try.

00:07:40.042 --> 00:07:42.962
The other orisha were
skeptical that someone so young

00:07:43.045 --> 00:07:46.632
could accomplish such
a difficult task,
but Oshun insisted.

00:07:46.716 --> 00:07:48.843
She turned herself into
a beautiful peacock

00:07:48.926 --> 00:07:51.596
and flew towards
Olofin's distant home.

00:07:51.679 --> 00:07:54.307
As she flew, her feathers
began to fall off,

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:56.559
and as she passed the fiery sun,

00:07:56.642 --> 00:07:58.686
her feathers smoldered away.

00:07:58.769 --> 00:08:01.063
She flew and flew and flew

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:03.691
until she didn't think
she could fly anymore,

00:08:03.774 --> 00:08:04.984
but she finally reached Olofin.

00:08:05.067 --> 00:08:07.987
By the time she
collapsed at their feet,

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:10.156
Oshun's feathers were all gone.

00:08:10.239 --> 00:08:14.118
Instead of a beautiful peacock,
she was an ugly vulture.

00:08:14.202 --> 00:08:16.829
Olofin was touched
by Oshun's sacrifice,

00:08:16.913 --> 00:08:18.915
by her courage and persistence,

00:08:18.998 --> 00:08:21.334
and agreed to send the
rains back to the world.

00:08:21.417 --> 00:08:25.087
For her troubles, Olofin
told Oshun that she alone

00:08:25.171 --> 00:08:27.924
as a vulture could communicate
directly with them.

00:08:28.007 --> 00:08:30.051
After this tale,
humans can commune

00:08:30.134 --> 00:08:32.720
with the orisha through
Eshu, But anyone who wants

00:08:32.803 --> 00:08:35.806
to reach Olofin must
go through Oshun.

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:39.018
Given her beauty and proximity
to the supreme creator,

00:08:39.101 --> 00:08:43.064
it's no surprise that Oshun
was one of the wives of Shango,

00:08:43.147 --> 00:08:46.442
the woman-loving orisha
of thunder and lightning.

00:08:46.525 --> 00:08:50.905
Together, they produced
Ibeji, a pair of sacred twins.

00:08:50.988 --> 00:08:53.950
To this day, twins are
special to the Yoruba

00:08:54.033 --> 00:08:56.702
and mistreating them is
said to bring about misfortune.

00:08:56.786 --> 00:09:00.039
In history, Shango was one
of the first Yoruba kings

00:09:00.122 --> 00:09:02.375
who ruled from the
ancient city Oyo

00:09:02.458 --> 00:09:04.710
and was closely
related to Oduduwa.

00:09:04.794 --> 00:09:08.130
In mythology, Shango is
one of many who sprang

00:09:08.214 --> 00:09:11.008
from the body of
the orisha Yemoja.

00:09:11.092 --> 00:09:13.511
The orisha Shango
briefly occupied

00:09:13.594 --> 00:09:17.431
the body of the mortal king,
but also took other forms

00:09:17.515 --> 00:09:19.183
throughout his immortal life.

00:09:19.267 --> 00:09:23.145
The human King Shango's
reign was harsh and violent.

00:09:23.229 --> 00:09:25.564
Legend says that he
was so relentless

00:09:25.648 --> 00:09:27.567
in his search for
power through magic

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:29.318
that when he finally
obtained dominion

00:09:29.402 --> 00:09:32.321
over thunder and lightning,
he accidentally used it

00:09:32.405 --> 00:09:36.158
to destroy his palace with
his wife and children inside.

00:09:36.242 --> 00:09:39.537
Dismayed, Shango fled his
kingdom and hung himself,

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:42.123
but his mighty power
was not forgotten.

00:09:42.206 --> 00:09:45.459
Shango was deified as an
orisha after his death

00:09:45.543 --> 00:09:48.296
or should I say that his
identity as the orisha

00:09:48.379 --> 00:09:50.840
was discovered after
the king's death.

00:09:50.923 --> 00:09:53.009
Either way, King Shango's spirit

00:09:53.092 --> 00:09:56.554
didn't follow the typical
path of other dead mortals.

00:09:56.637 --> 00:09:59.557
Someone who lived a long
good life could expect

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.601
to join the ancestors
in Orun after they died.

00:10:02.685 --> 00:10:06.355
If it was part of Olofin's plan,
they might be reincarnated.

00:10:06.439 --> 00:10:08.983
Unlike Hinduism's
karmic reincarnation,

00:10:09.066 --> 00:10:12.194
where a soul can return in
any number of random forms

00:10:12.278 --> 00:10:13.904
based on their behavior
in a previous life,

00:10:13.988 --> 00:10:16.824
atunwaye is the belief
that you will be

00:10:16.907 --> 00:10:19.535
reborn into your own family.

00:10:19.618 --> 00:10:21.370
Your actions in this life

00:10:21.454 --> 00:10:23.664
determine your family's
future fortune.

00:10:23.748 --> 00:10:27.168
So if you misbehave, you won't
come back as a cockroach.

00:10:27.251 --> 00:10:30.546
You'll be born into poverty
as your great-grandson.

00:10:30.629 --> 00:10:33.382
These stories and beliefs
from Yoruba mythology

00:10:33.466 --> 00:10:36.802
are a small part of a
colorful, dynamic culture

00:10:36.886 --> 00:10:39.430
that goes back
almost 2000 years.

00:10:39.513 --> 00:10:43.351
They are preserved in
Isese, a Yoruba philosophy,

00:10:43.434 --> 00:10:47.271
way of life and religion, which
is still practiced by many.

00:10:47.355 --> 00:10:49.857
Today, there are roughly
50 million people,

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:53.736
spread between parts of
Nigeria, Benin, Ghana, and Togo,

00:10:53.819 --> 00:10:54.987
who speak the Yoruba language.

00:10:55.071 --> 00:10:58.449
Yoruba and Isese
influence can be seen

00:10:58.532 --> 00:11:00.242
in Caribbean spiritual practices

00:11:00.326 --> 00:11:02.536
like Santeria and Candomble.

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:05.873
But now that you know a
bit more about atunwaye,

00:11:05.956 --> 00:11:08.834
the intervening orisha
and supreme Olofin,

00:11:08.918 --> 00:11:10.836
I'm willing to bet that
you'll start to notice

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:13.923
West African influence
all over modern media.

00:11:14.006 --> 00:11:15.800
But believe me when I say

00:11:15.883 --> 00:11:17.259
I've barely scratched
the surface

00:11:17.343 --> 00:11:18.844
of Yoruba mythology here.

00:11:18.928 --> 00:11:21.097
So comment below with
your favorite stories

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:24.558
or figures from Yoruba lore
and don't forget to thank Eshu

00:11:24.642 --> 00:11:26.644
before you hit send.

00:11:26.727 --> 00:11:28.437
Before you go, if
you're a fan of this show,

00:11:28.521 --> 00:11:30.606
there is a new
series on PBS Origins

00:11:30.689 --> 00:11:33.401
called "Rogue History"
that we know you'll enjoy.

00:11:33.484 --> 00:11:35.319
There is a mythology
surrounding pirates

00:11:35.403 --> 00:11:37.029
that has infiltrated
our culture,

00:11:37.113 --> 00:11:38.864
but how much of it is true?

00:11:38.948 --> 00:11:41.575
"Rogue History" examines
pirate lore to unravel

00:11:41.659 --> 00:11:43.786
historical myths,
unearth lost narratives,

00:11:43.869 --> 00:11:46.580
and introduce you to
little-known characters.

00:11:46.664 --> 00:11:48.958
It's the pirate history
you were never told.

00:11:49.041 --> 00:11:50.876
Check out the link in
the description below.

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:54.004
Accessibility provided by the
U.S. Department of Education.

